    "0f3723bd-eca6-4770-92ef-a59c8af5afd6": {
      "uuid": "0f3723bd-eca6-4770-92ef-a59c8af5afd6",
      "idea": "Electrostatic clothes trap particulates; 'clean' by discharge and brushing, no water.",
      "description": "Garments use charged fibers to capture dirt/pathogens, which are then released by neutralizing the charge for waterless cleaning by shaking/brushing, ensuring hygiene.",
      "feasibility_rationale": "Significant technical hurdles exist in creating a practical, reliable, safe, and effective system for actively charging and discharging fibers within a garment, and ensuring broad particulate (including microbial) capture and subsequent release.",
      "feasibility_score": 2,
      "novelty_rationale": "Using actively controlled electrostatic fields within clothing to attract particulates and then release them for waterless cleaning by discharge and brushing is a highly unconventional and inventive concept for garment care.",
      "novelty_score": 4,
      "potential_impact_rationale": "Could provide a completely waterless method for removing particulate matter. However, its impact on overall hygiene is moderate unless it effectively removes microbes and is combined with other methods for non-particulate soils.",
      "potential_impact_score": 3,
      "key_assumptions": [
        "Clothing fibers can be embedded with a system that allows them to be reliably, safely, and repeatedly charged to an effective level and then fully discharged on demand.",
        "The generated electrostatic forces will be strong enough to capture and retain a wide range of relevant particulates, including dust, allergens, and potentially microbes, from the environment and skin.",
        "Neutralizing the charge will allow for near-complete release and removal of these trapped particles through simple mechanical actions like shaking or brushing."
      ],
      "key_next_steps_to_validate_or_develop": [
        "Model and prototype basic electrostatically active textile swatches to assess their particle capture (various sizes, types) and release efficiency upon charge neutralization.",
        "Investigate viable low-power, safe, and durable mechanisms for on-garment charging and discharging, considering user interface and garment integration.",
        "Assess the limitations of this method, particularly its efficacy on biological contaminants (e.g., bacteria, viruses) versus inert dust, and how it might address non-particulate soiling (e.g., body oils, sweat residues)."
      ],
      "major_challenges_roadblocks": [
        "Developing a practical, lightweight, and durable embedded power source and circuitry for actively charging and discharging fibers within clothing without compromising comfort or safety.",
        "Ensuring effective capture of a broad range of particle sizes and types, including microbes, and then achieving efficient and complete release upon discharge.",
        "This method primarily addresses particulate matter and may not be effective for removing absorbed liquids, sweat, oils, stains, or odors, thus limiting its scope for complete hygiene."
      ]
    },
